@node Preliminaries
@chapter Introduction and Preliminaries

This chapter explains what GuCu is, how to read this documentation,
how to call the GuCu interface, and how to report bugs.  It then
introduces the contents of the remaining chapters.

@node Intro
@section Introduction to Curses, Guile, and GuCu

@cindex overview of GuCu

Ncurses is the core terminal interface library for @acronym{GNU}/Linux
and OpenBSD.  It provides the ability to do terminal programming in a
terminal-independent fashion.  Its routines for color, function key
mapping, symbols, pads, and input processing free the programmer from
having to know the specifics of the underlying terminal hardware or
emulation.  

Guile is a scripting language used on the GNU systems.  It is a
dialect of the language Scheme, a member of the Lisp family of
languages.  Guile provides a small core functionality, and additional
functionality can be provided by loading @emph{libraries}.  In Guile,
libraries are also refered to as @emph{modules}.

GuCu is a Guile library.  It is an implementation of an application
programming interface (@acronym{API}) that provides a rich set of
commands for terminal-based interfaces.  It relies on the
@acronym{GNU} Ncurses library to provide the underlying terminal
functionality, while trying to present an API that is more consistent
with Scheme programming practice.

GuCu actually include two separate APIs: the @emph{core} API and the
@emph{raw} API.  The core API tries to present an interface that is
familiar to Scheme programmers; the raw API, however, tries to present
an interface that is as close as possible to the C-language Ncurses
interface.  Of the two, the core interface will be used in the
tutorial and the examples.  The raw interface is provided for those
familiar with using Ncurses in C.

@node Bugs
@section Problems and bugs

@cindex reporting bugs
@cindex bug reports
@cindex suggestions, reporting
If you have any difficulty with the GuCu library or think that you've
discovered a bug, please report it.  Always remember to consult this
manual before you send off a bug report.  Maybe the answers are
already contained herein.

When reporting a bug, do try to create a small script that reproduces
the problem, if possible.  Then, check the version of the GuCu library
you are using and the version of Ncurses that it uses.  Assuming that
the package built and installed correctly, both of these version
numbers, as well as other pertinent information, are output by the
program @code{gucuinfo} provided by this distribution.  Also, provide
details about the platform on which the program is being run.

With this information compiled, send an e-mail to
@email{spk121@@yahoo.com}, and I'll see if I can help.  (Please be
advised that I'm just some skinny schmuck living in Los Angeles that
does this for fun, so my responsivness and accuracy vary.)

On the other hand, I'm fully aware that probably no one will read this
manual or use this package.  I'm so sure of it that the first person
to send me an e-mail containing the phrase ``the GuCu manual says give
me a dollar'' will get a rumpled dollar sent to them via U.S. mail.

Seriously, if there were a pressing need for this, why would I have to
write my own library?

Anyhoo@enddots{}

@node Manual
@section Using this manual

@cindex examples, understanding
(The text of this section is taken nearly verbatim from the @code{m4}
manual.  Thanks, guys!)

This manual contains a number of examples of Guile and Ncurses input
and output, and a simple notation is used to distinguish input, output
and error messages from Scheme code.  Examples are set out from the
normal text, and shown in a fixed width font, like this

@comment ignore
@example
This is an example of an example!
@end example

To distinguish input from output, all output from a program is
prefixed by the string @samp{@result{}}, and all error messages by the
string @samp{@error{}}.  When showing how command line options affect
matters, the command line is shown with a prompt @samp{$ @kbd{like
this}}.  Thus:

@comment ignore
@example
$ @kbd{command line to invoke a program}
Example of input line
@result{}Output line from a program
@error{}and an error message
@end example

If the sequence @samp{^D} appears in an example, it indicates the end
of the input file.  The sequence @samp{@key{NL}} refers to the newline
character.  Some of these examples are self-contained Guile scripts,
and you can run them with similar results by saving them as text files
and executing them as scripts.

As each of the procedures in GuCu is described, a
prototype of the function will be shown, giving descriptive names to
the arguments, e.g.,

@deffn Procedure example string [#:key keyvar] [argument] 

This is a template for a call to the procedure @var{example}.  (There
is not really a procedure named @code{example}.  This is just an
example.)  It requires at least one argument, @var{string}.  The
brackets around @var{keyvar} and @var{argument} show that these
arguments are optional.  The optional variable @var{keyvar} requires
the keyword @code{#:key} to precede it if it is to be used.  Note that
while required arguments like @var{string} and optional arguments like
@var{argument} must be passed to the procedure in the order indicated,
keyword arguments like @var{keyvar} can be passed to the procedure in
any order.
@end deffn

Please recall that, for Scheme, a @emph{variable} can contain
a @emph{value} or a @emph{procedure}.  A procedure is called with
@emph{arguments}. 

@node Invoking GuCu
@chapter ``Invoking'' the GuCu library

A library is not @emph{invoked} so much as it is @emph{imported};
however, the @cite{@acronym{GNU} Coding Standards} insist that a
manual have a section with the word ``invoke'' in the title.

Depending on the application, a program would import one of the
following forms of the library. 

@table @code

@item (gucu raw)
This loads a version of the GuCu library that tries to present an
@acronym{API} that is as close as possible to the C-language Ncurses
@acronym{API}.  This form of the library is described in Chapter FIXME. 

@item (gucu core)
This loads a version of the GuCu library that presents a more
verbose, Scheme-like @acronym{API} that covers the functionality
provided by Ncurses.  A tutorial on the use of this library is in
Chapter FIXME, and a reference of the functions in this library is in
Chapter FIXME.
@end table

A program should not import both the @code{raw} and the @code{core}
versions of the library into the same namespace.  Use one or the
other.

@node Installation Problems
@section Installation Problems

It is quite likely that after you install the library and try to use
it or include it into a program, it will fail to work.

If you installed it without laboriously reading through the INSTALL
document, you probably will get a result like the following.

@example
$ @kbd{guile}
guile> @kbd{(use-modules (gucu core))}
@error{}standard input:1:1: no code for modules (gucu core)
@error{}ABORT: (misc-error)
@end example

If you want a quick fix, try this.
@example
$ @kbd{LD_LIBRARY_PATH=/usr/local/lib GUILE_LOAD_PATH=/usr/local/share/guile/site guile}
guile> @kbd{(use-modules (gucu core))}
@end example

Did it work?  If so, add @env{LD_LIBRARY_PATH} and @env{GUILE_LOAD_PATH}
to your @file{.profile} or @file{.bashrc} and you should be ready to go.

The most likely reason for why you receive this error has to do with
installation directories.  Most distributions install pre-packaged
distro-supplied programs in a specific directory tree, such as
@file{/usr}, while most user-compiled programs end up in a different
tree, such as @file{/usr/local}.  If Guile was prepackaged to appear
in @file{/usr}, for example, it would expect that new Guile modules
appear in @file{/usr/share/guile/site}.  If the GuCu module was
compiled using the default settings, the library tree is probably
located in @file{/usr/instead/share/guile/site}, instead.  When
searching for modules, Guile may not search into that directory by
default.

Guile searches a list of paths for modules when @code{use-module} is
called.  To see the list, display the contents of the
@code{%load-path} variable.

@example
$ @kbd{guile}
guile> @kbd{(display %load-path)}
@result{}(/usr/share/guile/site /usr/share/guile/1.8 /usr/share/guile .)
@end example

If you find that the GuCu library has not been installed in one of the
directories in the @code{%load-path}, there are two basic solutions.

@enumerate
@item
Add the install location of the GuCu library to the @code{%load-path}.
@item
Reinstall the GuCu library into a directory in Guile's
@code{%load-path}.
@end enumerate

There are a couple of methods to get Guile to add new directories to
the load path, short of a recompilation.

One, the program can append the location of @code{(gucu)} to the
@code{%load-path}.  Two, the environment variable
@env{GUILE_LOAD_PATH} can be updated to append the location of
libraries before Guile is invoked.  Three, later versions of Guile can
be called with the @option{-L} option, to add a library path at
runtime.
 
Once this hurdle has been fixed, you may then get another error such
as

@example
@error{}libguile-gucu.so: cannot open shared object file: No such file or directory
@end example

This error comes from the Libtool LTDL library which Guile uses to
load binary libraries.  When this error occurs, it indicates that the
shared object file either does not exist or is not in the set of paths
that the LTDL searches when trying to dynamically link.  In this case
you will have to modify the run-time linker path used by LTDL.  This
can be done by appending the location of libguile-gucu.so to the
environment variable @env{LD_LIBRARY_PATH}.

@node Ncurses versions
@section Versions of Ncurses

Depending on how it was compiled, the Ncurses library will be one of
four flavors.  It may or may not contain functions for wide
characters, and it may or may not contain functions to assist in
debugging curses functions.  When using the Ncurses library in C,
using a wide character function or debugging function with a version
of Ncurses that does not support it would probably result in a
link-time error.

Typically, one can determine the compilation options of the Ncurses
library from the resulting filename of the library.

@table
@item @file{libncurses} or @file{libcurses}
This library does not have wide character support and support for
run-time debugging functions.
@item @file{libncursesw} or @file{libcursesw}
This library has wide character support.
@item @file{libncurses_g} or @file{libcurses_g}
This library has support for run-time debugging.
@item @file{libncursesw_g} or @file{libcursesw_g}
This library has support for wide characters and for run-time
debugging.
@end table

The GuCu library is written so that wide-character and debugging
procedures are always available in the @acronym{API}.  If they are
used with an Ncurses library that does not support them, however, an
error will result.

